00:58:42.612 --> 00:58:42.992
Seeker: Um...

00:58:42.992 --> 00:58:46.112
DM: [laughs] So the only thing that's not claimed is Art, Art.

00:58:46.512 --> 00:58:48.372
DM: Seeker, are you taking the ring of warded mind?

00:58:48.972 --> 00:58:49.212
Delwath: Sure.

00:58:49.892 --> 00:58:50.132
DM: Okay.

00:58:50.172 --> 00:58:50.432
Delwath: Sure.

00:58:57.152 --> 00:58:59.092
Seeker: So things to put in front of the mirror.

00:59:00.592 --> 00:59:01.152
Seeker: I disagree.

00:59:02.232 --> 00:59:03.812
Seeker: Just kidding. [laughs]

00:59:03.832 --> 00:59:05.492
Kenzo: I think the moaning, the moaning lamp, right?

00:59:06.012 --> 00:59:06.692
Delwath: That feels like a-

00:59:07.232 --> 00:59:09.992
Seeker: Well, like what do we wanna find out as opposed to what do we wanna put in front of it?

00:59:10.012 --> 00:59:12.012
Delwath: So, okay. I, I didn't actually...

00:59:12.572 --> 00:59:15.912
Delwath: I wrote like a little what has happened so far in Delwath's,

00:59:16.972 --> 00:59:21.932
Delwath: from Delwath's viewpoint, and then I decided I didn't like the tone of it, so I didn't send, I didn't share it or anything.

00:59:22.392 --> 00:59:22.592
Delwath: But

00:59:23.692 --> 00:59:26.312
Delwath: I think the story so far is actually pretty simple, right?

00:59:26.312 --> 00:59:27.512
Delwath: Agatha has been out,

00:59:28.472 --> 00:59:28.712
Delwath: um,

00:59:30.452 --> 00:59:31.732
Delwath: you know, Agatha stole the heart.

00:59:33.932 --> 00:59:34.832
Delwath: We don't really know

00:59:35.852 --> 00:59:37.812
Delwath: why, but presumably for revenge.

00:59:37.812 --> 00:59:38.892
Delwath: Mostly for revenge.

00:59:39.552 --> 00:59:39.972
Seeker: Against?

00:59:41.452 --> 00:59:41.672
Delwath: Uh, hmm?

00:59:42.192 --> 00:59:42.812
Seeker: Against whom?

00:59:44.352 --> 00:59:45.872
Delwath: Everybody who didn't

00:59:47.392 --> 00:59:48.672
Delwath: lose when Shemut was-

00:59:49.152 --> 00:59:49.412
Seeker: Mm. Mm. Mm

00:59:49.412 --> 00:59:50.892
Delwath: ... supposedly taking over, right?

00:59:51.692 --> 00:59:54.492
Delwath: Um, and all kinds of bad stuff came out, right?

00:59:54.512 --> 00:59:57.852
Delwath: Like, there was the skeletal hobgoblin from the very beginning of the campaign.

00:59:58.772 --> 01:00:03.532
Delwath: Um, there was like those hyenas and scorpions that were turning huge and ferocious.

01:00:04.492 --> 01:00:05.612
Delwath: Um, there was like-

01:00:05.612 --> 01:00:06.432
Seeker: Yeah, where did those come from?

01:00:08.432 --> 01:00:10.632
Delwath: The hobgoblin. Remember we killed the hobgoblin?

01:00:11.072 --> 01:00:12.052
Seeker: Mm-hmm.

01:00:12.052 --> 01:00:15.392
Delwath: But presumably just randomness, not like driven by any-

01:00:15.472 --> 01:00:15.512
Seeker: Mm.

01:00:15.512 --> 01:00:17.712
Delwath: They didn't seem like it was tied to anything, right?

01:00:18.272 --> 01:00:23.412
Delwath: Then in Ravenshold, there was the demon who was ensorceling the hyenas to create gnolls and sending them about.

01:00:24.052 --> 01:00:28.432
Delwath: We don't really know who was be- the first question we don't know the answer to is who was behind the demon.

01:00:30.012 --> 01:00:30.612
Seeker: Agatha.

01:00:31.192 --> 01:00:31.372
Delwath: Agatha.

01:00:31.372 --> 01:00:31.572
Seeker: Who was-

01:00:31.592 --> 01:00:37.232
Delwath: Who was behind, who created the demon who was creating the gnolls and causing all the trouble?

01:00:38.532 --> 01:00:42.312
Seeker: Right. But didn't we learn about Ag- Agatha at that, in that adventure?

01:00:42.952 --> 01:00:44.712
Delwath: Yeah, but she wasn't the one who create...

01:00:44.712 --> 01:00:48.112
Delwath: She traded the, she traded the scroll

01:00:49.352 --> 01:00:51.612
Delwath: that summons the demon for the,

01:00:52.492 --> 01:00:52.872
Delwath: um-

01:00:53.452 --> 01:00:53.772
Seeker: Scepter

01:00:53.772 --> 01:01:04.031
Delwath: ... scepter So somebody found the Scepter of Command in Carson and traded it to Agata in exchange for this ability

01:01:04.031 --> 01:01:04.872
Delwath: to create nulls.

01:01:06.072 --> 01:01:10.232
Drikod: And maybe something else. I thought one of the hypotheses why Agata was

01:01:12.172 --> 01:01:15.352
Drikod: transacted with somebody, um, to steal the heart.

01:01:15.352 --> 01:01:18.932
Drikod: Like, she got something out of it, revenge, chaos, magic items.

01:01:19.532 --> 01:01:22.332
Drikod: But somebody else wanted the heart gone for a nefarious...

01:01:22.332 --> 01:01:23.332
Drikod: I thought someone or-

01:01:23.352 --> 01:01:24.912
Delwath: Maybe. Yeah, maybe

01:01:24.912 --> 01:01:25.152
Drikod: ... that, um-

01:01:25.152 --> 01:01:25.852
Delwath: I had forgotten that, but

01:01:25.852 --> 01:01:34.352
Drikod: ... that it wasn't, like, personal revenge, but, like, s- somebody was using Agata as part of their larger plan, likely connected to the other things of sowing chaos-

01:01:34.952 --> 01:01:35.092
Delwath: Right

01:01:35.092 --> 01:01:36.792
Drikod: ... for some, some end.

01:01:38.272 --> 01:01:38.572
Delwath: Um,

01:01:46.012 --> 01:01:51.132
Delwath: so I mean, the other s- thing that Agata did is somebody worked with her to, to, um,

01:01:52.272 --> 01:01:54.612
Delwath: she was the instrument of getting Siri off the throne.

01:01:56.132 --> 01:01:56.192
Drikod: Mm.

01:01:56.432 --> 01:01:57.352
Delwath: The queen, right?

01:01:57.672 --> 01:01:58.132
Unknown: Right.

01:01:59.572 --> 01:02:01.952
Delwath: But again, we don't think she was behind that.

01:02:01.952 --> 01:02:03.732
Delwath: She wa- it wasn't, like, her plan, right?

01:02:04.412 --> 01:02:04.652
Drikod: Mm-hmm.

01:02:04.712 --> 01:02:12.312
Kenzo: Okay, so this is a part, Tim, where, um, explain to us, um, like how Dunmar decides kings again.

01:02:12.312 --> 01:02:14.012
Kenzo: Like, how does that work? Like, how's the-

01:02:14.472 --> 01:02:14.772
DM: So the rulers of Dunmar-

01:02:14.792 --> 01:02:16.172
Unknown: So you're saying it's not a royal bloodline

01:02:16.172 --> 01:02:18.512
DM: ... has, known as Samrats-

01:02:18.512 --> 01:02:19.392
Unknown: [laughs]

01:02:19.392 --> 01:02:22.652
DM: ... are, um, historically chosen by the gods.

01:02:23.852 --> 01:02:26.432
DM: So every, every...

01:02:26.432 --> 01:02:27.232
DM: When a new,

01:02:28.752 --> 01:02:28.932
DM: um,

01:02:30.112 --> 01:02:33.792
DM: when a new Samrat is crowned, one of the first things that they do is,

01:02:34.812 --> 01:02:35.032
DM: um,

01:02:36.052 --> 01:02:36.932
DM: is choose... Well,

01:02:38.492 --> 01:02:40.412
DM: it, it varies the timing, right?

01:02:40.412 --> 01:02:42.392
DM: It depends on how old they are, et cetera, et cetera.

01:02:42.392 --> 01:02:46.372
DM: But so usually sometimes during their, during their reign they choose an heir.

01:02:47.692 --> 01:02:49.432
DM: Well, the gods choose an heir for them.

01:02:50.012 --> 01:02:50.032
DM: Um.

01:02:50.052 --> 01:02:50.592
Unknown: Which gods?

01:02:51.292 --> 01:02:52.292
DM: The Dunmari gods.

01:02:53.172 --> 01:02:54.992
Unknown: Like Bishma, Laka?

01:02:55.072 --> 01:02:56.512
DM: Yeah. Yeah.

01:02:56.532 --> 01:02:57.032
Unknown: Do they, like,

01:02:58.192 --> 01:02:59.772
Kenzo: get together and have a meeting?

01:02:59.812 --> 01:03:01.652
DM: Well, exactly how this happen, how,

01:03:02.632 --> 01:03:08.572
DM: how do the gods make their will known to their subjects is complicated, right?

01:03:08.572 --> 01:03:13.892
DM: So there's room for error, and it doesn't always go exactly as planned,

01:03:14.972 --> 01:03:22.032
DM: and there's, like, a whole bunch of ceremonies and rituals associated with this that are supposed to identify the person who the gods choose.

01:03:22.892 --> 01:03:25.152
DM: But for a while, it seemed it worked pretty well.

01:03:26.272 --> 01:03:26.552
DM: Um,

01:03:29.712 --> 01:03:32.832
DM: then at some point, like, the original dynasty where...

01:03:32.832 --> 01:03:34.312
DM: which was, like, the, the first

01:03:35.272 --> 01:03:45.292
DM: r- run of people who were chosen basically as far as anybody could tell directly by the gods ended, and there was the dynasty of the horse, which was, uh, basically there

01:03:45.292 --> 01:03:54.992
DM: was this horse, uh, essentially a mortal horse that was thought to be the spirit of Chidya, one of the gods, who would select the next Samrat.

01:03:56.572 --> 01:03:58.932
DM: And for a while, that was the kind of...

01:03:58.932 --> 01:04:00.732
DM: seen as the instrument of the gods' will.

01:04:01.912 --> 01:04:04.892
DM: And then since the Great War, it's much less clear, right?

01:04:04.892 --> 01:04:05.612
DM: Like, there's no,

01:04:06.592 --> 01:04:09.292
DM: um, there's been a lot of debate about...

01:04:11.352 --> 01:04:17.912
DM: There are definitely people, like Hovdart and a few others in the East, who are not convinced that the

01:04:19.692 --> 01:04:22.952
DM: current rulers of Dunmar are in fact chosen by the gods.

01:04:24.732 --> 01:04:30.132
DM: But it's tricky because the, the Samrat is also the supreme leader of the

01:04:30.912 --> 01:04:31.832
DM: religious hierarchy.

01:04:34.232 --> 01:04:36.092
DM: And, you know, the gods can

01:04:37.012 --> 01:04:40.232
DM: p- produce miracles obviously, so there's...

01:04:40.252 --> 01:04:43.112
DM: If things go really off the rails, they can step in and be like,

01:04:44.192 --> 01:04:48.852
DM: you know, appear as a giant head in the sky and be like, "No, this is wrong.

01:04:49.692 --> 01:04:51.352
DM: He is not going to be your king."

01:04:52.092 --> 01:04:56.872
DM: But that requires a huge amount of, of energy, and so they don't usually do that, right?

01:04:56.872 --> 01:05:01.672
DM: 'Cause gods can't... It's very difficult for the gods to directly interact with the world.

01:05:05.152 --> 01:05:05.592
DM: So

01:05:06.792 --> 01:05:09.852
DM: it's, you know, politics to a certain extent now.

01:05:10.832 --> 01:05:11.972
DM: But tinged with a,

01:05:13.212 --> 01:05:13.511
DM: you know...

01:05:14.992 --> 01:05:16.912
DM: It's sort of like how the pope is chosen, right?

01:05:16.912 --> 01:05:17.852
DM: Like, you know,

01:05:19.332 --> 01:05:26.392
DM: everybody says it's the go- uh, you know, chosen by the gods, but, like, there's politics involved, and it's not entirely clear

01:05:28.372 --> 01:05:32.532
DM: currently the role of the gods and the role of politics.

01:05:36.252 --> 01:05:38.752
Drikod: Drikod finds human politics insane.

01:05:43.352 --> 01:05:44.292
Delwath: Yeah, Delwath too.

01:05:44.292 --> 01:05:45.272
Drikod: [laughs]

01:05:45.272 --> 01:05:52.912
Delwath: They're all so shortsighted. [laughs]

01:05:52.932 --> 01:05:54.112
Unknown: Whereas the elves who are

01:05:55.872 --> 01:05:56.272
Unknown: doing what anymore?

01:05:56.292 --> 01:05:57.832
Delwath: Such short time horizons.

01:05:57.832 --> 01:06:00.892
Kenzo: [laughs] Where are the elves, and what are they doing right now?

01:06:01.752 --> 01:06:05.592
Delwath: The elves almost sacrificed their entire race to save humanity from Shemite.

01:06:06.632 --> 01:06:06.812
Unknown: Mm.

01:06:10.572 --> 01:06:13.092
Delwath: Now they're mostly... Well, I know Delwath has no idea what they're doing now.

01:06:13.092 --> 01:06:15.452
Delwath: He has not seen another elf since the Great War.

01:06:16.592 --> 01:06:16.752
Unknown: Mm.

01:06:20.052 --> 01:06:20.512
Unknown: That's rough.

01:06:21.752 --> 01:06:28.032
Delwath: But leading up to the Great War, there was a huge debate in elven society about whether to do something even if it meant

01:06:29.152 --> 01:06:31.792
Delwath: losing a lot or whether to retreat even further into

01:06:32.892 --> 01:06:33.232
Delwath: their,

01:06:35.032 --> 01:06:38.972
Delwath: you know, isolated, hidden cities, and Delwath was on the side of do something.

01:06:40.472 --> 01:06:42.572
Seeker: Don't they have, like, extra planar summer homes and stuff?

01:06:43.492 --> 01:06:43.792
Delwath: Yeah.

01:06:45.632 --> 01:06:46.432
Delwath: They all got killed.

01:06:47.172 --> 01:06:47.492
Unknown: Mm.

01:06:49.492 --> 01:06:54.552
Delwath: I mean, who knows? There's probably elves hidden in their extra planar summer homes who have been around for 5,000 years.

01:06:58.112 --> 01:06:59.992
DM: Anyway, so sorry, what was the point of this?

01:07:00.892 --> 01:07:01.752
Seeker: You were trying to figure out-

01:07:02.412 --> 01:07:04.132
Seeker: Trying to disagree about what to show the mirror.

01:07:04.392 --> 01:07:04.572
Unknown: Um-

01:07:04.672 --> 01:07:05.272
Seeker: Ah, okay.

01:07:05.312 --> 01:07:12.072
Delwath: So where I was going with all this is I think the most useful things to show the mirror, to Tim's point, the mirror's probably not gonna tell us, like,

01:07:14.572 --> 01:07:17.672
Delwath: what happened around something, right?

01:07:18.152 --> 01:07:22.992
Delwath: Like, it's not gonna be as useful if we show it, like, the tea kettle with the hope of,

01:07:24.432 --> 01:07:28.692
Delwath: of it showing us scenes from the life of a t- of a...

01:07:28.692 --> 01:07:32.232
Delwath: I mean, it's gonna show us scenes from the life of the tea kettle, right?

01:07:32.232 --> 01:07:35.692
Delwath: Like when it was created, somebody pouring water into it-

01:07:35.732 --> 01:07:35.732
Seeker: Mm-hmm

01:07:35.732 --> 01:07:38.032
Delwath: ... with no other details, like that kind of thing, right?

01:07:38.552 --> 01:07:44.212
Delwath: It's not gonna be like what was going around, what was going on while the tea kettle was sitting there.

01:07:45.872 --> 01:07:49.032
Delwath: So I was trying to think of items that actually we care about.

01:07:49.032 --> 01:07:52.492
Delwath: I mean, one could be the, one could be the soul mirror, but only if we think...

01:07:52.512 --> 01:07:53.972
Delwath: I mean, we're gonna destroy it, right?

01:07:54.452 --> 01:07:57.292
Delwath: So the only reason we should show it to the mirror is if we think it's-

01:07:57.932 --> 01:07:59.152
Unknown: We're gonna destroy the soul mirror?

01:07:59.152 --> 01:08:00.672
Delwath: Not the mir- the lantern, the lantern.

01:08:00.672 --> 01:08:00.852
Unknown: Oh, the lantern.

01:08:00.852 --> 01:08:01.692
Delwath: The creepy lantern-

01:08:01.892 --> 01:08:02.292
Unknown: Yeah. Yeah. Yeah

01:08:02.292 --> 01:08:03.712
Delwath: ... filled with the souls of the dead.

01:08:03.772 --> 01:08:03.912
Unknown: Yeah.

01:08:03.912 --> 01:08:04.052
Delwath: Right?

01:08:04.712 --> 01:08:08.512
Kenzo: That's what I was thinking, 'cause we- because we're gonna destroy it, may as well learn something about it before we do.

01:08:09.612 --> 01:08:11.812
Delwath: But I don't know if it's gonna tell us anything about...

01:08:11.812 --> 01:08:13.952
Delwath: It's just gonna be depressing stories of

01:08:15.012 --> 01:08:20.432
Delwath: innocent people being tormented to death and having their souls sucked into this lantern probably.

01:08:21.072 --> 01:08:25.852
Kenzo: But what if it's, like, interesting things, like this hobgoblin or, like, the orc, you know?

01:08:25.852 --> 01:08:28.972
Delwath: It could be, right. I was wondering if there's a way,

01:08:29.732 --> 01:08:35.432
Delwath: if... I mean, I was thinking about the scorpions, those dried scorpions, which we still are convinced have more to them than

01:08:36.992 --> 01:08:40.552
Delwath: just normal dried scorpions, because it would be a way of finding out what they were.

01:08:41.192 --> 01:08:44.952
Delwath: The other thing I was thinking about was, is there any item that would help us

01:08:45.792 --> 01:08:47.132
Delwath: learn more about what

01:08:49.032 --> 01:08:50.392
Delwath: is going on?

01:08:50.392 --> 01:08:54.172
Delwath: Like, I feel like there's something going on that we haven't been able to pierce yet, right?

01:08:54.192 --> 01:08:54.332
Delwath: Like,

01:08:55.532 --> 01:08:58.112
Delwath: who was trading with Agata?

01:08:58.872 --> 01:09:01.432
Delwath: Why did somebody want to, um,

01:09:03.232 --> 01:09:06.591
Delwath: why did somebody want to make an army of gnolls, right?

01:09:06.591 --> 01:09:07.612
Delwath: What's Grash up to?

01:09:08.572 --> 01:09:11.812
Delwath: Uh, why is Grash, like, trying to kill the Uzgosh?

01:09:12.912 --> 01:09:13.532
Unknown: What's Uzgosh?

01:09:14.092 --> 01:09:15.992
Delwath: Uzgosh were the peaceful farmer folks.

01:09:16.092 --> 01:09:16.752
Seeker: Yeah, they're the nice folks.

01:09:16.772 --> 01:09:17.672
Unknown: Oh, right. Right.

01:09:17.692 --> 01:09:18.132
Delwath: Right.

01:09:18.132 --> 01:09:18.332
Seeker: Do we know if-

01:09:18.332 --> 01:09:19.672
Delwath: Who is Grash reporting to, right?

01:09:19.692 --> 01:09:21.712
Delwath: Like, we caught Grash talking to somebody.

01:09:22.192 --> 01:09:23.452
Seeker: Oh, crap. You're right.

01:09:23.452 --> 01:09:25.572
Seeker: Do we know of any connection between Grash and Agata?

01:09:27.772 --> 01:09:28.252
Delwath: Well,

01:09:29.892 --> 01:09:33.132
Delwath: no, but it would be interesting to find out if there was one.

01:09:33.492 --> 01:09:36.092
Kenzo: And what was Grash looking for? Grash was also looking for some items.

01:09:36.712 --> 01:09:38.552
Delwath: Ma- maybe he was looking for the Scepter of Command.

01:09:39.052 --> 01:09:39.352
Unknown: Probably.

01:09:39.392 --> 01:09:41.312
Seeker: Yeah. We hypothesized that.

01:09:41.312 --> 01:09:44.332
Seeker: Should we put the Scepter of Command in front of the mirror?

01:09:44.332 --> 01:09:45.092
Delwath: We already did.

01:09:45.332 --> 01:09:45.952
Seeker: We already did that already? We did that already.

01:09:45.972 --> 01:09:45.972
Delwath: Yeah.

01:09:45.972 --> 01:09:46.012
Seeker: Okay.

01:09:46.012 --> 01:09:50.192
Delwath: That's how we know that... We know it was found in Carson, and then Agata-

01:09:52.132 --> 01:09:52.192
Unknown: Oh.

01:09:52.192 --> 01:09:52.472
Delwath: What?

01:09:54.072 --> 01:09:56.312
Kenzo: I have some things I need to share in character, but we got to wait for Wellby.

01:09:56.312 --> 01:10:00.412
Delwath: [laughs]

01:10:00.432 --> 01:10:02.552
Seeker: Um, now what about the clockwork farm animals, guys?

01:10:02.872 --> 01:10:04.592
Kenzo: Oh, wait. Really quickly, back to the scepter.

01:10:04.592 --> 01:10:04.912
Unknown: Tim?

01:10:04.952 --> 01:10:05.552
Delwath: Yep?

01:10:05.632 --> 01:10:09.132
Kenzo: Uh, who... Like, you know how we were watching, like, somebody use the scepter to, like, kind of-

01:10:09.152 --> 01:10:09.152
Delwath: Yep

01:10:09.152 --> 01:10:10.932
Kenzo: ... bring kingdoms... Like, who was that?

01:10:11.472 --> 01:10:12.232
Unknown: Was it a human or not?

01:10:12.232 --> 01:10:13.652
Seeker: Uh, was that what?

01:10:14.312 --> 01:10:16.952
Kenzo: Holding the, the scepter, like, you know, in, in the mirror.

01:10:16.972 --> 01:10:20.472
DM: It, it seemed to be, like, a, like, a leader of Drankor.

01:10:20.472 --> 01:10:22.312
DM: You, you don't know who exactly.

01:10:22.312 --> 01:10:22.792
Unknown: But a human.

01:10:23.392 --> 01:10:24.112
Seeker: A human, yeah.

01:10:27.532 --> 01:10:31.992
Delwath: Um, the other thing that I was just thinking about that we don't...

01:10:35.632 --> 01:10:39.032
Delwath: Ah, right. There was the rumor of an ancient artifact that Agata is looking for,

01:10:39.892 --> 01:10:41.612
Delwath: the one that blocks out the...

01:10:41.612 --> 01:10:44.092
Delwath: Some sort of cloak that, like, blocks out the gods.

01:10:47.292 --> 01:10:47.812
Delwath: That Sin-

01:10:48.652 --> 01:10:49.172
Delwath: Sinta

01:10:51.332 --> 01:10:53.272
Delwath: thought was what Agata was after.

01:10:55.252 --> 01:10:57.092
Seeker: Cloak that blocks out the gods.

